    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Project KO11 Is Proof That Geely Wants To \"Knock Out\" Range Rover, Launches Next Year in Europe\nHondaJet Classic Jets Can Now Be Flown in Europe With APMG S Upgrades\n13 photos\nPhoto: HondaJet\nAt the end of 2025, American plane maker Honda Aircraft Company announced an upgrade package for the relatively old HondaJet Classic aircraft lineup in a bid to give it modern capabilities, in line with the newer Elite S. This week, the upgrade solution, titled APMG S, got cleared for use in the European Union as well, following type validation from American and Mexican regulators.\nAPMG stands for Advanced Performance Modification Group S, and it’s an upgrade package meant to make the Classic jets, initially produced from 2015 to 2018, both safer to fly and better at their job, in line with present-day expectations.\nFor the Classic, that includes features the plane didn’t originally have, including upgraded avionics software, tweaked hardware, and faster processing speeds. On top of everything, the planes gain the ability to be upgraded further in the future as new technologies emerge.\nAPMG also brings to the table the Advanced Steering Augmentation System (ASAS) that, on the HondaJet Elite S, increases handling and crosswind capabilities, and an increase in maximum takeoff weight (MTOW) of 300 pounds (136 kg) - the total now stands at 10,900 pounds (4,944 kg).\nFinally, new graphical weight and balance features are included in the mix, giving pilots the ability to quickly calculate takeoff and landing weights using loading and fuel data.\nAccording to HondaJet, a plane running the APMG S package comes with improved performance numbers. The plane’s maximum speed is now 422 knots (486 mph/728 kph), the max cruise altitude it can reach is 43,000 feet (13,100 meters), while the range, when flying with four people on board and equipped with fuel reserves, is now 1,563 miles (2,517 km).\nFor HondaJet operators on the Old Continent, word of the European Union Aviation Safety Agency (EASA) clearing the upgrade package must feel like a blessing, but, in all fairness, though, I was kind of expecting HondaJet to give us an update on the new version of the airplane called Echelon.\nFirst presented during the 2023 National Business Aviation Convention and Exhibition (NBAA-BACE) in Las Vegas, the newcomer is supposed to be the most advanced of its kind yet, with a pair of Williams International engines giving it the ability to fly at speeds of up to 833 mph (1,340 kph) and reach distances of up to 3,000 miles (4,828 km).\nA perfect fit, then, to link Point Arena, California, to West Quoddy Head, Maine, the greatest distance between two mainland points in the U.S.. And that should make it the world's only transcontinental single-pilot light jet currently available.\nWe know the upcoming jet will be available in two configurations, serving different operator needs: one crew and ten passengers, or two crew and nine passengers. It’s likely the new aircraft will be chasing type certification in 2028.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHondaJet Honda apmg\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Ferrari, Lamborghini, Porsche: It's Time To Start Thinking About Rockets",
